# function run(list, t) { let last = ""; for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) { const [x, y] = list; last = x + "|" + y; } return last + "|" + (t & 1); }
# print(run(own, 3));
# -- end of source
# A pattern destructures by index, without the iterator protocol, only if its operand is certainly a pristine array.
# This array has an iterator method of its own assigned to it, so Link does not claim that it stays pristine, and the pattern takes what that iterator yields.
# Fault injection, testing=AssumePermanentlyPristineIterables: the operand of a pattern is assumed to be an array that Link claims stays pristine.
# Link's claim is checked against the object where the iteration opens, in every build. The check tests one bit of the object's cell and crashes with a named error if it fails.
# So the injected fault is caught loudly, and no element is read in place of what the iterator yields.
# RUN: run-in-shell mechanisms=all
# CHECK: 4|5|1
# CHECK-NEXT: exited with 0
# RUN(LIE): run-in-shell mechanisms=all testing=AssumePermanentlyPristineIterables
# LIE: bun: internal AOT error: type check failed at {{*}} (run)
# LIE-NEXT: in the result of an operation
# LIE: crashed with
